🔹 Phase 2: Deploy JupyterHub in Minikube (via Helm)

The recommended way is using the Zero-to-JupyterHub Helm chart.

1. Install Helm
brew install helm

2. Add JupyterHub Helm repo
helm repo add jupyterhub https://jupyterhub.github.io/helm-chart/
helm repo update

3. Create a namespace for JupyterHub
kubectl create namespace jhub

4. Create a config.yaml for JupyterHub

Example config.yaml:
proxy:
  secretToken: "super-secret-token"

hub:
  config:
    JupyterHub:
      authenticator_class: dummy
      dummy_authenticator:
        password: "password"   # all users can login with this

singleuser:
  image:
    name: jupyter/minimal-notebook
    tag: latest

5. Install JupyterHub with Helm
helm upgrade --cleanup-on-fail \
  jhub jupyterhub/jupyterhub \
  --namespace jhub \
  --create-namespace \
  --version=3.3.7 \
  --values config.yaml

  helm install jhub jupyterhub/jupyterhub \
  --namespace jhub \
  --create-namespace \
  --version=4.2.0 \
  --values config.yaml



(The version number may change — helm search repo jupyterhub will show the latest.)
6. Check deployment
kubectl get pods -n jhub


Wait until all pods are Running.

7. Expose JupyterHub service

Forward port from the proxy service:

kubectl --namespace jhub port-forward svc/proxy-public 8080:80


Now you can access JupyterHub at 👉 http://127.0.0.1:8080

## Stopping the h jupyterhub

🔹 1. Stop JupyterHub (temporarily)

This just scales pods down but keeps config/data.

helm uninstall jhub -n jhub


This removes the JupyterHub pods/services, but if you used a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC), user data is preserved.

🔹 2. Restart JupyterHub (reinstall)

To bring it back:

helm install jhub jupyterhub/jupyterhub \
  --namespace jhub \
  --create-namespace \
  --version=4.2.0 \
  --values config.yaml

🔹 3. Stop and restart only pods (lighter way)

If you don’t want to uninstall/reinstall the release, you can just restart pods:

Stop (delete) pods:

kubectl delete pods -n jhub --all


Kubernetes will automatically recreate them based on the Helm release.

Restart deployment (proxy or hub):

kubectl rollout restart deployment hub -n jhub
kubectl rollout restart deployment proxy -n jhub

🔹